{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The protocol’s safety hinges on a generation registry that tracks completed-evidence counts per generation. Because the process crashes with no terminal record, the registry can lag behind the actual storage: a generation may contain evidence that was written but not yet registered. Reclamation that trusts the registry’s zero count for that generation will reclaim the storage, silently displacing completed evidence that existed on disk but was not durably counted.","target":"502c8e38e908953cc5e3319f1abd038a25123ea3a9a7a0cf60142861ed5c8a3a"},{"attack":true,"case":"The protocol substitutes a consensus lease and proof-of-absence for direct knowledge of storage contents. After a crash with no terminal record, the lease metadata and the physical workspace diverge: the crashed process may have already written evidence that the lease nameset does not yet reflect, or the proof-of-absence may authorize reclamation based on stale consensus state. Reclamation then proceeds on the physical workspace using metadata that no longer matches reality, silently displacing completed evidence that was committed under the previous lease but not yet visible to the linearizable query.","target":"8574eff8a79f64f713f7fff39a4743ca1ea49e858a1c5b4491e6b948b6aedf02"},{"attack":true,"case":"The mechanism garbage-collects orphaned unmerged leaves that are not ancestors of any evidence node. After a crash with no terminal record, a fully-written evidence node may exist in the DAG but not yet appear in the durably recorded set of merged evidence nodes. Under the protocol’s own GC rule, that node is an unmerged leaf without an ancestor relationship in the last durable snapshot, so reclamation will garbage-collect it, silently displacing completed evidence that was present in storage but not yet merged.","target":"b0128fb8afcd98f9ea6861f30f9f04e02a9899607e213d2f4b8fa60c83a3cf25"},{"attack":true,"case":"The protocol treats reclamation as deterministic replay from a barrier. Because the crash leaves no terminal record, the audit log may be incomplete relative to the actual workspace state. Replay regenerates an earlier state and overwrites storage, which can silently displace completed evidence that was produced after the last durably logged input but before the crash. A protocol that merely recomputes state does not prevent displacement; it only makes it recoverable, violating the required guarantee.","target":"ca19e09074d69ca070208272641ea4f1017c34cd9fcd48d683e0f8043fd6dc89"}]}